# block_expr_reject.tkr — the §9 E Block-B (expression position, ruling 7) REJECT host. Two illegal
# forms live one per namespace under src/, and the single failing build reports every diagnostic it
# finds, one per scenario below. A Block-B in VALUE position must end in a value expression: a trailing
# statement that yields no value (an assignment) is rejected, and a block that DIVERGES (a trailing
# `return`) is rejected. It has no successful build to sanitize/run.

Feature: §9 E Block-B reject — the compiler rejects a Block-B in value position with no trailing value

  # (trailing_assign) the trailing statement is an assignment, which yields no value.
  Scenario: trailing_assign — a Block-B ending in an assignment is rejected
    When compilation fails
    Then diagnostic = "a block used as a value must end in a value expression, not a statement"

  # (trailing_return) the block diverges via a trailing `return`, so it never falls through to a value.
  Scenario: trailing_return — a Block-B that diverges is rejected
    When compilation fails
    Then diagnostic = "a block used as a value cannot diverge"
